---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Productive Waiting - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="productive-waiting">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				AI coding tools can take anywhere from a few seconds to several minutes to complete a
				task. Longer tasks like implementing a feature across multiple files, running tests, or
				refactoring a module can take a while.
			</p>

			<p>
				The temptation is to check your phone, or just wait passively. But
				this dead time is actually some of the most valuable time in your workflow. You are the
				domain expert on your project; the AI is handling the mechanical work, which frees you
				to do the thinking work that only you can do.
			</p>

			<h3>You Are Still the Domain Expert</h3>

			<p>
				No matter how capable the AI gets at writing code, it does not understand the business,
				the users, or the broader context of what you're building. It doesn't know about upcoming features or bugs.
				You are still the product owner and the domain expert on your feature.
			</p>

			<h3>What to Do While AI Works</h3>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="tip" title="Review the last thing it produced">
					<p>
						If the AI just finished a previous task, use this time to review that output. Read
						the diff, check the logic, make sure it matches what you asked for. Catching issues
						early saves you from compounding problems across tasks.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Write requirements for the next task">
					<p>
						Think ahead. Draft the prompt or requirements for the next piece of work so you can
						hand it off the moment the current task finishes. This keeps your pipeline moving
						and avoids the start-stop pattern that kills momentum.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Research and context gathering">
					<p>
						Look up documentation for an API you'll need later. Read through a pull request
						from a teammate. Check the ticket backlog for context on upcoming work. This kind
						of research directly improves the quality of what you'll ask the AI to do next.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-2">
						You can also hand this off to a second AI chat. While your main agent implements, ask
						another one read-only questions like "where is rate limiting handled in this codebase?"
						or "does this library support X?", or have it help you plan your next feature: draft
						requirements or a rough approach so it's ready to hand off the moment the current task
						finishes. Investigation and planning are low-risk because nothing is being changed, and
						the results are ready when you are.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Think about edge cases and testing">
					<p>
						While the AI writes the happy path, think about what could go wrong. What inputs
						haven't been considered? What do you need to test when it's done? Having a mental
						list of edge cases ready makes your review better and your testing more thorough.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Update documentation or tickets">
					<p>
						Update your Jira tickets, write notes in Confluence, or document a decision you
						just made. Documentation as we talked about is now more important than ever, and one of the most valuable things in your repository going forward.
					</p>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<h3>The Shift in Your Role</h3>

			<p>
				Working with AI changes the shape of your day. You spend less time typing code and more
				time directing, reviewing, and planning. This is closer to how a
				senior engineer or tech lead already works: setting direction, reviewing output, and
				making sure the right thing gets built.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="info" title="The practical takeaway">
				<p>
					AI handles the typing. You handle the thinking. When the AI is working, stay engaged between tasks and get the most value out of it.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
